course: dictionary
lesson: 10644
title: Words that stretch beyond imagination
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words describe things that are so big, small or extreme that your brain struggles to picture them. Unimaginably is one of these words. It means to a degree so great, small or extreme that it is very hard or impossible to imagine. «Humans have existed for only about 300,000 years, so that is an unimaginably long time away.» The clue is the comparison: people have been around for a short time compared with this huge gap, so the word stresses how far beyond everyday life the size is.
explain: The word is built from parts you may know. Un- means not, and imagine means to picture in your mind. So unimaginably is about something you cannot picture. Try the swap check: put in a word such as "very" and the sentence still works, but unimaginably tells the reader that the size goes past everyday experience.
explain: Scientists use this word when numbers become too big or too tiny to picture. «In 1974 Stephen Hawking calculated that black holes should slowly leak tiny amounts of energy and, over unimaginably long times, evaporate.» The clue is "over ... long times": the time is so long that no one can really picture it.
fact: Unimaginably means to a degree so great, small or extreme that it is very hard or impossible to imagine.
fact: Un- means not, so unimaginably means in a way you cannot imagine.
fact: Scientists use it for huge distances in space, tiny atoms and very long times.
fact: In stories and history it can describe things like unimaginably cruel or unimaginably brave.
fact: The word tells the reader that the size or strength goes beyond everyday experience.
exercise: In the sentence about black holes, which word shows that the time is too long to picture? | leak | over | *unimaginably | evaporate
exercise: «Humans have existed for only about 300,000 years, so that is an unimaginably long time away.» What does unimaginably mean here? | A little bit | Happy and calm | Very quickly | *So great that it is very hard to imagine
exercise: Maya says unimaginably means "a little bit". What is the right meaning? | Very quietly | *So extreme that it is very hard to imagine | In a sensible way | A little bit
test: The word unimaginably is built from un- and imagine. What does un- mean here? | Before | Very | Again | *Not
test: Which of these is the best example of something unimaginably tiny? | A bus | A football | *An atom | A mountain
exam: What does unimaginably mean? | Quickly and loudly | Happily and calmly | Next to something else | *Beyond what we can easily picture
